  "result": "Comparative Analysis of Large Language Model Inference Serving Systems: A Performance Study of vLLM and HuggingFace TGI (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17593v1)\nciteturn6734view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6731view0\",\"lineno\":219}); Total lines: 573\nL168: ### 3.2 Models and Configurations\nL169: \nL170: We evaluated three LLaMA-2 model variants:\nL171: \nL172:   1. 1.\nL173: \nL174: LLaMA-2-7B: 7 billion parameters, single GPU deployment\nL175: \nL176:   2. 2.\nL177: \nL178: LLaMA-2-13B: 13 billion parameters, single GPU deployment\nL179: \nL180:   3. 3.\nL181: \nL182: LLaMA-2-70B: 70 billion parameters, tensor parallel across 4 GPUs\nL183: \nL184: For the 70B model, we used tensor parallelism to distribute the model across multiple GPUs. Both frameworks were configured with FP16 precision to balance performance and quality.\nL185: ### 3.3 Workload Characteristics\nL186: \nL187: We designed benchmark workloads to reflect realistic deployment scenarios:\nL188: \nL189: #### 3.3.1 Request Patterns\nL190: \nL191:   * •\nL192: \nL193: Low Concurrency (1-10 users): Simulates interactive chatbot scenarios\nL194: \nL195:   * •\nL196: \nL197: Medium Concurrency (10-50 users): Typical production load\nL198: \nL199:   * •\nL200: \nL201: High Concurrency (50-200 users): Stress test for peak demand\nL202: #### 3.3.2 Input/Output Characteristics\nL203: \nL204:   * •\nL205: \nL206: Prompt lengths: Sampled from Poisson distribution (mean=512 tokens)\nL207: \nL208:   * •\nL209: \nL210: Generation lengths: Sampled from Poisson distribution (mean=256 tokens)\nL211: \nL212:   * •\nL213: \nL214: Temperature: 0.7 with top-p sampling (p=0.9)\nL215: \nL216: #### 3.3.3 Dataset\nL217: \nL218: We used prompts from the ShareGPT dataset, which contains real user-AI conversations, ensuring realistic input distributions. Each benchmark run consisted of 1,000 requests to ensure statistical significance.\nL219: ### 3.4 Metrics\nL220: \nL221: We measured the following performance indicators:\nL222: \nL223:   1. 1.\nL224: \nL225: Throughput: Tokens generated per second across all requests\nL226: \nL227:   2. 2.\nL228: \nL229: Latency: Time from request submission to completion\nL230: \nL231:   3. 3.\nL232: \nL233: Time to First Token (TTFT): Latency until first token generation\nL234: \nL235:   4. 4.\nL236: \nL237: Time per Output Token (TPOT): Average time to generate each token\nL238: \nL239:   5. 5.\nL240: \nL241: GPU Utilization: Percentage of GPU compute used\nL242: \nL243:   6. 6.\nL244: \nL245: GPU Memory Usage: Peak and average memory consumption\nL246: \nL247:   7. 7.\nL248: Request Throughput: Requests completed per second\nL249: \nL250: For latency metrics, we report p50 (median), p95, and p99 percentiles to capture tail behavior critical for user experience.\nL251: ### 3.5 Benchmarking Protocol\nL252: \nL253: Each experiment followed this protocol:\nL254: \nL255:   1. 1.\nL256: \nL257: Warm-up phase: 100 requests to stabilize system state\nL258: \nL259:   2. 2.\nL260: \nL261: Measurement phase: 1,000 requests with metrics collection\nL262: \nL263:   3. 3.\nL264: \nL265: Cool-down period: 30 seconds between experiments\nL266: \nL267:   4. 4.\nL268: \nL269: Repetition: 3 runs per configuration, reporting median values\nL270: \nL271: We monitored GPU metrics using nvidia-smi at 1-second intervals and application-level metrics through framework APIs.\nL272: \nL273: ## 4 Results\nL274: ### 4.1 Throughput Performance\nL275: \nL276: Figure 1 shows token throughput as a function of concurrent requests across different model sizes. Key findings:\nL277: \nL278: LLaMA-2-7B: vLLM achieves peak throughput of 15,243 tokens/sec at 100 concurrent requests, compared to TGI’s 4,156 tokens/sec—a 3.67x advantage. This gap widens to 24x under extreme load (200 concurrent requests) where TGI’s throughput degrades while vLLM maintains near-peak performance.\nL279: LLaMA-2-13B: The throughput advantage narrows to 2.8x (vLLM: 8,934 tokens/sec vs TGI: 3,187 tokens/sec at optimal concurrency) as memory pressure increases and both systems become more constrained.\nL280: \nL281: LLaMA-2-70B: With tensor parallelism across 4 GPUs, vLLM maintains 2.1x higher throughput (3,245 vs 1,544 tokens/sec). The reduced gap reflects communication overhead in distributed execution, which affects both systems similarly.\nL282: Scaling Behavior: vLLM’s throughput scales more linearly with concurrency due to efficient memory management. TGI shows throughput saturation beyond 50 concurrent requests for the 7B model, indicating memory bottlenecks.\nL283: ### 4.2 Latency Analysis\nL284: \nL285: Table 1 presents latency percentiles under medium concurrency (25 concurrent users):\nL286: Table 1: Latency metrics (seconds) at 25 concurrent users\nL287:  | TTFT  | Total Latency  | TPOT\nL288: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL289: Model  | vLLM  | TGI  | vLLM  | TGI  | vLLM  | TGI\nL290: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL291: 7B - p50  | 0.24  | 0.18  | 4.82  | 5.91  | 0.019  | 0.023\nL292: 7B - p95  | 0.89  | 0.45  | 9.34  | 14.28  | 0.037  | 0.058\nL293: 7B - p99  | 1.42  | 0.71  | 14.18  | 23.47  | 0.056  | 0.094\nL294: 13B - p50  | 0.31  | 0.22  | 8.45  | 10.24  | 0.033  | 0.041\nL295: 13B - p95  | 1.12  | 0.58  | 16.89  | 24.56  | 0.067  | 0.098\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparative Analysis of Large Language Model Inference Serving Systems: A Performance Study of vLLM and HuggingFace TGI (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17593v1)\nciteturn6734view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6731view0\",\"lineno\":444}); Total lines: 573\nL387: #### 5.1.2 Batching Strategy\nL388: \nL400: TGI: More consistent latency, lower throughput\nL401: #### 5.1.3 Kernel Optimization\nL402: \nL403: Both systems use custom CUDA kernels, but with different optimization focuses:\nL404: \nL405: vLLM: Optimizes primarily for the PagedAttention operation, with kernels designed for non-contiguous memory access patterns.\nL406: \nL407: TGI: Focuses on optimizing standard attention and includes extensive quantization support (GPTQ, AWQ, EETQ).\nL408: \nL409: ### 5.2 Workload Suitability\nL410: \nL411: Based on our findings, we provide the following recommendations:\nL412: #### 5.2.1 Use vLLM when:\nL413: \nL414:   * •\nL415: \nL416: High throughput is critical: Batch processing, document analysis, offline evaluation\nL417: \nL418:   * •\nL419: \nL420: Concurrent users are high: Multi-tenant serving, API services\nL421: \nL422:   * •\nL423: \nL424: Memory is constrained: Need to serve larger models or longer contexts\nL425: \nL426:   * •\nL427: \nL428: GPU utilization matters: Cost optimization through better resource usage\nL429: #### 5.2.2 Use TGI when:\nL430: \nL431:   * •\nL432: \nL433: Low latency TTFT is crucial: Interactive chat applications, real-time assistance\nL434: \nL435:   * •\nL436: \nL437: Predictable latency matters: SLA-bound services requiring consistent response times\nL438: \nL439:   * •\nL440: \nL441: Quantization is needed: Resource-constrained deployments requiring INT8/INT4\nL442: \nL443:   * •\nL444: \nL445: HuggingFace ecosystem integration: Easy model loading from Hub, extensive model support\nL446: \nL447:   * •\nL448: \nL449: Production features: Built-in monitoring, distributed inference, extensive configuration options\nL450: ### 5.3 Hybrid Strategies\nL451: \nL452: Organizations might consider hybrid deployments:\nL453: \nL454:   * •\nL455: \nL456: Request routing: Direct interactive queries to TGI, batch jobs to vLLM\nL457: \nL458:   * •\nL459: \nL460: Time-based switching: Use TGI during peak hours for latency, vLLM during batch processing windows\nL461: \nL462:   * •\nL463: \nL464: Model-specific choices: Smaller models on TGI for interactivity, larger models on vLLM to manage memory\nL465: ### 5.4 Limitations and Future Work\nL466: \nL467: Our study has several limitations that suggest directions for future work:\nL468: \nL469:   1. 1.\nL470: \nL471: Model diversity: We focused on LLaMA-2 models. Performance characteristics may differ for other architectures (Mistral, Falcon, GPT variants).\nL472: \nL473:   2. 2.\nL474: \nL475: Quantization: We did not evaluate quantized models (INT8, INT4), which significantly affect memory and performance.\nL476: \nL477:   3. 3.\nL478: Hardware variations: Results are specific to A100 GPUs. Newer architectures (H100, AMD MI300) may show different patterns.\nL479: \nL480:   4. 4.\nL481: \nL482: Specialized workloads: Long-context scenarios (32K+ tokens) and structured generation (JSON, code) warrant separate analysis.\nL483: \nL484:   5. 5.\nL485: \nL486: Cost analysis: We did not perform detailed cost modeling accounting for cloud pricing, though throughput differences have clear cost implications.\nL487: Future work should address these gaps and track the rapidly evolving landscape of LLM serving systems.\nL488: ## 6 Related Systems and Trade-offs\nL489: \nL490: Beyond vLLM and TGI, the ecosystem includes several other frameworks worth considering:\nL491: \nL492: TensorRT-LLM: NVIDIA’s solution provides the highest throughput through extensive kernel optimization and quantization. However, it requires per-model compilation and has a steeper learning curve. Best for organizations with NVIDIA-focused infrastructure and engineering resources.\nL493: DeepSpeed-FastGen: Introduces dynamic SplitFuse for better handling of variable-length generation. Particularly strong for workloads with high variance in generation lengths. Tighter integration with DeepSpeed training pipeline.\nL494: \nL495: LMDeploy: Developed by MMRazor team, focuses on mobile and edge deployment with quantization and pruning support.\nL496: \nL497: OpenLLM: Emphasizes developer experience and rapid prototyping, sacrificing some performance for ease of use.\nL498: The choice of framework should align with organizational priorities, existing infrastructure, and specific application requirements.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond Surface-Level Similarity: Hierarchical Contamination Detectionfor Synthetic Training Data in Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17602v1)\nciteturn6734view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6731view1\",\"lineno\":96}); Total lines: 276\nL87: Second, we perform embedding space clustering using DBSCAN to identify synthetic samples that cluster near benchmark embeddings in the representation space. Third, we analyze distributional characteristics: benchmark clusters should exhibit distinct geometric properties (higher density, tighter variance) compared to typical training data. Samples satisfying $\\text{sim}_{\\text{emb}}>\\tau_{2}$ AND cluster membership AND distributional anomaly detection are flagged.\nL88: Level 3: Reasoning Pattern Detection. For reasoning tasks, we extract chain-of-thought traces from model outputs and compute reasoning pattern similarity using structured comparison.\nL89: \nL90:  | $$\\text{sim}_{\\text{reasoning}}(x_{i}^{s},x_{j}^{b})=\\alpha\\cdot\\text{StructSim}+\\beta\\cdot\\text{StepSim}+\\gamma\\cdot\\text{ArgSim}$$  |  | (4)\nL91: where StructSim measures logical structure similarity, StepSim compares reasoning steps, and ArgSim evaluates argument patterns. Samples exhibiting suspiciously similar reasoning chains to benchmark exemplars beyond what would be expected from common problem-solving strategies are flagged.\nL92: Level 4: Performance-Cliff Detection. We leverage model behavior on benchmark variants. If a model performs significantly better on original benchmarks versus semantically equivalent paraphrased versions, this suggests memorization rather than understanding. For each benchmark, we generate K paraphrased variants and compute:\nL93: \nL94:  | $$\\Delta_{\\text{cliff}}=\\text{Acc}(B_{\\text{orig}})-\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\text{Acc}(B_{\\text{para}}^{(k)})$$  |  | (5)\nL95: Statistical significance testing (paired t-test) determines whether $\\Delta_{\\text{cliff}}$ exceeds natural variation, providing an aggregate contamination signal.\nL96: ### 3.3 Implementation Details\nL97: We implement Level 1 using the official Min-K% Prob codebase with K=20%. For Level 2, we use the all-mpnet-base-v2 sentence transformer, DBSCAN with $\\epsilon=0.15$ and min_samples=5, and multivariate Gaussian distribution fitting. Level 3 extracts CoT traces using regular expressions to parse reasoning steps, computing similarity using weighted Jaccard similarity on n-gram sets.\nL98: Level 4 generates 5 paraphrased variants per benchmark instance using GPT-4 with instructions to preserve semantic meaning while varying lexical content, selecting high-quality paraphrases validated by BLEURT scores $>0.7$ (cite48†Sellam et al. 2020 ).\nL99: ## 4 Experiments\nL100: ### 4.1 Experimental Setup\nL101: \nL102: Benchmarks. We evaluate on three widely-used benchmarks: (1) MMLU (cite49†Hendrycks et al. 2020 ) with 200 samples in abstract algebra, sociology, and US history, (2) GSM8K (cite50†Cobbe et al. 2021 ) with 150 grade-school math problems, and (3) HumanEval (cite51†Chen et al. 2021 ) with 100 Python programming tasks. These benchmarks represent diverse reasoning capabilities and contamination risks.\nL103: Contamination Scenarios.\nL104: We construct four controlled contamination scenarios with increasing subtlety: (S1) Token-Level: synthetic data contains exact benchmark questions, (S2) Paraphrase: GPT-4 generated semantic paraphrases of benchmark questions preserving meaning, (S3) Semantic: synthetic data clusters conceptually near benchmarks (e.g., similar math problem types, related programming tasks) without direct paraphrase, generated using topic-guided synthesis, (S4) Reasoning-Pattern: models generate synthetic data exhibiting similar reasoning chains to benchmark exemplars through few-shot prompting with benchmark-style reasoning patterns.\nL105: Ground truth contamination labels determined through expert annotation by three ML researchers specializing in LLM evaluation.\nL106: Baseline Methods. We compare against: (1) N-gram Overlap (13-gram matching following GPT-3 (cite32†Brown et al. 2020 )), (2) Min-K% Prob (cite41†Deng et al. 2024b ), (3) Embedding Similarity (cosine similarity with sentence-transformers), (4) LLM Decontaminator (cite36†Yang et al. 2023 ) combining embedding search with GPT-4 semantic judgment, (5) CDD (cite52†Dong et al. 2024 ) using output distribution peakedness.\nL107: Evaluation Metrics. We report precision, recall, and F1 score for contamination detection. Statistical significance assessed using McNemar’s test with $p<0.05$.\nL108: ### 4.2 Results\nL109: \nL110: Table cite53†1 presents detection performance across contamination scenarios. On token-level contamination (S1), all methods perform well, with our hierarchical framework achieving F1=0.96 comparable to Min-K% Prob (0.945) and marginally better than n-gram overlap (0.925). This validates that our framework does not sacrifice performance on easier detection tasks.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond Surface-Level Similarity: Hierarchical Contamination Detectionfor Synthetic Training Data in Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17602v1)\nciteturn6734view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6731view1\",\"lineno\":169}); Total lines: 276\nL162:   * Dong et al. (2024) Dong, H.; Xiong, W.; Goyal, D.; Pan, R.; Diao, S.; Zhang, J.; Shum, K.; and Zhang, T. 2024. Contamination detection via output distribution analysis. arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.11234.\nL163:   * Duan et al. (2024) Duan, Y.; Kong, A.; Zhou, X.; Ma, Y.; Song, D.; Li, B.; and Shmatikov, V. 2024. Do membership inference attacks work on large language models? In Proceedings of CCS, 2345–2359.\nL164:   * Golchin and Surdeanu (2024) Golchin, S., and Surdeanu, M. 2024. Data contamination quiz: A tool to detect and quantify contamination in large language models. In Proceedings of EMNLP, 4512–4527.\nL165:   * Hendrycks et al. (2020) Hendrycks, D.; Burns, C.; Basart, S.; Zou, A.; Mazeika, M.; Song, D.; and Steinhardt, J. 2020. Measuring massive multitask language understanding. In Proceedings of ICLR.\nL166:   * Lee et al. (2023) Lee, K.; Ippolito, D.; Nystrom, A.; Zhang, C.; Eck, D.; Callison-Burch, C.; and Carlini, N. 2023. Deduplicating training data makes language models better. In Proceedings of ACL, 8424–8445.\nL167:   * Mitra et al. (2024) Mitra, A.; Del Corro, L.; Mahajan, S.; Codas, A.; Simoes, C.; Agarwal, S.; Chen, X.; Razdaibiedina, A.; Jones, E.; Agrawala, K.; et al. 2024. Orca-AgentInstruct: Agentic flows for synthetic data generation. Technical report, Microsoft Research.\nL168:   * Reimers and Gurevych (2019) Reimers, N., and Gurevych, I. 2019. Sentence-BERT: Sentence embeddings using Siamese BERT-networks. In Proceedings of EMNLP-IJCNLP, 3982–3992.\nL169:   * Sellam et al. (2020) Sellam, T.; Das, D.; and Parikh, A. P. 2020. BLEURT: Learning robust metrics for text generation. In Proceedings of ACL, 7881–7892.\nL170:   * Shi et al. (2024) Shi, W.; Ajith, A.; Xia, M.; Huang, Y.; Liu, D.; Blevins, T.; Chen, D.; and Zettlemoyer, L. 2024. Detecting pretraining data from large language models. In Proceedings of ICLR.\nL171:   * Sun et al. (2025) Sun, L.; Wang, H.; Zhang, Y.; and Li, M. 2025. Emperor’s new clothes: A systematic evaluation of data contamination mitigation strategies. In Proceedings of AAAI.\nL172:   * Touvron et al. (2023) Touvron, H.; Martin, L.; Stone, K.; Albert, P.; Almahairi, A.; Babaei, Y.; Bashlykov, N.; Batra, S.; Bhargava, P.; Bhosale, S.; et al. 2023. Llama 2: Open foundation and fine-tuned chat models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2307.09288.\nL173:   * Villalobos et al. (2022) Villalobos, P.; Sevilla, J.; Heim, L.; Besiroglu, T.; Hobbhahn, M.; and Ho, A. 2022. Will we run out of data? An analysis of the limits of scaling datasets in machine learning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2211.04325.\nL174:   * Wang et al. (2024) Wang, Y.; Kordi, Y.; Mishra, S.; Liu, A.; Smith, N. A.; Khashabi, D.; and Hajishirzi, H. 2024. Self-Instruct: Aligning language models with self-generated instructions. In Proceedings of ACL, 13484–13508.\nL175:   * White et al. (2024) White, C.; Dooley, S.; Roberts, M.; Pal, A.; Feuer, B.; Jain, S.; Night, C.; Phute, M.; Shwartz-Ziv, R.; Jain, N.; et al. 2024. LiveBench: A challenging, contamination-free LLM benchmark. arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.19314.\nL176:   * Wu et al. (2024) Wu, Z.; Qiu, L.; Ross, A.; Arik, S. O.; Chen, H.; Wang, X.; Yu, Y.; Dai, H.; Wang, X.; and Etzion, Y. 2024. Temporal contamination: A new challenge in LLM evaluation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2404.01234.\nL177:   * Xu et al. (2024) Xu, C.; Sun, Q.; Zheng, K.; Geng, X.; Zhao, P.; Feng, J.; Tao, C.; and Jiang, D. 2024. WizardLM: Empowering large language models to follow complex instructions. In Proceedings of ICLR.\nL178:   * Yang et al. (2023) Yang, K.; Swope, A. M.; Gu, A.; Chalamala, R.; Song, P.; Yu, S.; Godil, S.; Prenger, R. J.; and Anandkumar, A. 2023. LLM decontaminator: Detecting and mitigating contamination in LLM training data. arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.08780.\nL179: ## Appendix A Additional Experimental Details\nL180: ### A.1 Hyperparameter Selection\nL181: \nL182: Table cite55†3 details hyperparameters for each framework level, selected through grid search on a held-out validation set of 100 contaminated and 100 clean samples.\nL183: Table 3: Hyperparameters for each detection level.\nL184: Level  | Hyperparameter Settings\nL185: --- | ---\nL186: L1: Token-Level  | K=20%, $\\tau_{1}$=3.5 (log-prob)\nL187: L2: Semantic  | $\\tau_{2}$=0.75 (cosine), $\\epsilon$=0.15, min_samples=5\nL188: L3: Reasoning  | $\\alpha$=0.4, $\\beta$=0.3, $\\gamma$=0.3\nL189: L4: Performance  | K=5 variants, $p$-value threshold=0.05\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBehind the Screens: Uncovering Bias in AI-Driven Video Interview Assessments Using Counterfactuals (https://arxiv.org/html/2505.12114v2)\nciteturn6734view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6731view5\",\"lineno\":341}); Total lines: 541\nL320: MTDNN-Visual  | 0.009  | 0.010  | 0.000\nL321: MTDNN-10F  | 0.004  | 0.012  | 0.004\nL322: MTDNN-Visual-10F  | 0.006  | 0.016  | 0.005\nL323: MTDNN-Inv  | 0.009  | 0.004  | 0.005\nL324: MTDNN-Visual-Inv  | 0.008  | 0.010  | 0.002\nL325: MTDNN-Inv-Mod  | 0.012  | 0.012  | 0.016\nL326: MTDNN-Visual-Inv-Mod  | 0.017  | 0.008  | 0.008\nL327: TABLE V: Disparate impact scores for each model across gender, ethnicity, and age, based on interview score–based candidate selection using a top-$N$ threshold of $N=200$. A value of 1.0 indicates equal selection rates between protected and unprotected groups.\nL328: Model  | Protected Group\nL329: --- | ---\nL330: Gender  | Ethnicity  | Age\nL331: --- | --- | ---\nL332: Dataset  | 0.582  | 0.467  | 0.000\nL333: MTDNN  | 0.377  | 0.106  | 1.115\nL334: MTDNN-Visual  | 0.280  | 0.289  | 0.483\nL335: MTDNN-10F  | 0.260  | 0.365  | 0.239\nL336: MTDNN-Visual-10F  | 0.221  | 0.251  | 0.360\nL337: MTDNN-Inv  | 0.435  | 0.404  | 1.115\nL338: MTDNN-Visual-Inv  | 0.290  | 0.289  | 0.000\nL339: MTDNN-Inv-Mod  | 1.803  | 0.443  | 0.360\nL340: MTDNN-Visual-Inv-Mod  | 3.306  | 1.082  | 0.607\nL341: TABLE VI: Disparate impact scores for each model across gender, ethnicity, and age, based on candidate selection using an interview score threshold of $\\tau=0.75$. A disparate impact score of 1.0 indicates equal selection rates between protected and unprotected groups, corresponding to statistical parity—a common form of group fairness.\nL342: Model  | Protected Group\nL343: --- | ---\nL344: Gender  | Ethnicity  | Age\nL345: --- | --- | ---\nL346: Dataset  | 0.586  | 0.464  | 0.091\nL347: MTDNN  | 0.260  | 0.122  | 1.820\nL348: MTDNN-Visual  | 0.274  | 0.612  | 0.000\nL349: MTDNN-10F  | 0.222  | 0.243  | 0.000\nL350: MTDNN-Visual-10F  | 0.202  | 0.990  | 0.000\nL351: MTDNN-Inv  | 0.199  | 0.220  | 0.394\nL352: MTDNN-Visual-Inv  | 0.182  | 1.312  | 0.000\nL353: MTDNN-Inv-Mod  | 2.088  | 0.770  | 0.394\nL354: MTDNN-Visual-Inv-Mod  | 4.577  | 3.186  | 1.314\nL355: ### V-A Experiment 1: Personality Prediction Model Results\nL356: \nL357: In Experiment 1, we present the accuracy results of the MTDNN model and its variants, analyzing performance across different input modalities (e.g., visual, audio), the distribution of OCEANI scores across protected groups, and the presence of initial bias in these models. These results provide insight into potential biases in the dataset and model predictions, as well as general trends in OCEANI dimension scores.\nL358: First, we examine the prediction performance per OCEANI dimension and the average performance across demographic groups for the baseline MTDNN model, as shown in Table cite107†II on the left and right sides, respectively. These values are computed as (1 – mean absolute error), where higher values indicate better performance.\nL359: From these results, we make the following observations: (a) The MTDNN model achieves the highest prediction performance across all OCEANI dimensions and demographic groups compared to the other model variants. (b) Among models that use a single modality, performance ranks in the order: visual, audio, and text, with the visual-only model achieving the highest prediction performance and the text-only model the lowest, though the latter still achieves a (1 – MAE) score above 87% across all dimensions.\nL360: (c) There is a slight decrease in prediction performance when using only the first 10 frames of video, but performance remains high. (d) The GAN-inverted models (MTDNN-Inv and MTDNN-Visual-Inv) perform well, though slightly below their original counterparts. (e) Across all demographic groups, average prediction performance is comparable between majority and minority groups, with no large disparities observed.\nL361: To further investigate disparities in model outputs, we analyzed the distribution of interview scores across demographic groups, as shown in Table cite108†III and Figure cite109†3 .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAeroVerse: UAV-Agent Benchmark Suite for Simulating, Pre-training, Finetuning, and Evaluating Aerospace Embodied World Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2408.15511v2)\nciteturn6734view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6731view4\",\"lineno\":279}); Total lines: 580\nL274: Figure cite85†9 (d) illustrates the distribution of various actions, revealing that “MOVE-FORWARD” is considerably more prevalent than other actions. This observation is entirely logical, as the process of flying from the starting point to the endpoint involves primarily forward flight, with turns, ascents, and other maneuvers required to avoid obstacles and detours.\nL275: The average length of the action sequences depicted in Figure cite85†9 (e) is $82$, further emphasizing that our dataset focuses on long-distance drone flights in large-scale urban environments. This distinction marks the primary difference between this dataset and those associated with existing indoor scene datasets.\nL276: ### V-H Image Enhancement\nL277: To fully consider the challenges faced by drones in real-world complex environments, this study systematically addressed two key issues encountered during flight through data augmentation. First, regarding the image tilt caused by drone body jitter, we collect more diverse data on camera tilts in the environment.\nL278: Second, to address image blurring or pose drift due to jitter, we simulate the motion blur degradation process of drone images by constructing directional motion blur kernels (PSF) and applying convolutional operations. Based on the assumption of linear uniform motion, the algorithm first generates blur kernels of specified size and angle, ensuring energy conservation through normalization, and then employs two-dimensional discrete convolution to achieve image degradation.\nL279: This method effectively simulates typical motion blur caused by drone body jitter or pose drift, as shown in figure cite86†10 .\nL280: cite87†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 10: Figure (a) and (b) show the motion blur effects in the horizontal direction and the 45-degree direction, respectively.\nL281: ## VI Experiments\nL282: ### VI-A Baselines\nL283: Baselines Selection. Due to the current scarcity of research on aerospace-embodied world models, we evaluate several mainstream and representative 3D and 2D visual-language models. This assessment aims to explore their potential and limitations concerning the proposed aerospace-embodied downstream task datasets, thereby providing a preliminary foundation for future researchers in the field of aerospace-embodied intelligence.\nL284: While there are more 2D visual-language models available that are generally more mature, we focus on LLaVA[cite88†34 ], MiniGPT4[cite89†35 ], and BLIP2[cite90†36 ], categorizing them into $7B$ and $13B$ models based on parameter scales. Given the limited availability of open-source 3D visual-language models, we select only the 3D-LLM[cite44†10 ] as our research focus.\nL285: cite91†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 11: Specific modifications of visual-language models for aerospace embodied downstream tasks .\nL286: Baselines Modification. Among the selected baseline models, the 3D visual-language model can be applied to most of the defined downstream tasks; however, the 2D visual-language model cannot be directly utilized for testing due to a mismatch in input formats, as illustrated in Figure cite92†11 . Consequently, we modify the inputs and outputs of these models to align with the downstream tasks, as detailed below.\nL287: Notably, Aerospace Embodied Motion Decision represents the culmination of aerospace embodied tasks, achieving a closed loop of perception, cognition, and action for the UAV agent. Adjusting existing visual-language models presents challenges, and we will continue to explore this area in future.\nL288: Aerospace Embodied Scene Awareness. This task involves utilizing the location and environmental data captured by the drone as input to generate scene descriptions of the surrounding environment from multiple perspectives. However, the 2D visual-language model is inherently limited to processing images and does not directly account for environmental features.\nL289: To mitigate this limitation during testing, we modify the 2D visual-language model by providing it with four images captured from the drone’s perspectives: front, back, left, and right. After generating captions for these images using descriptive prompts, we concatenate the four captions to produce the output for environmental observation, as illustrated in Figure cite92†11 (a).\n"
}
